<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<TimeML xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="TimeML_1.2.1.xsd">
  <DOCID>CURATION_USER</DOCID>
  <TIMEX3 tid="t0" type="DATE" value="2017-05-17T00:00:00" functionInDocument="CREATION_TIME" temporalFunction="false">2017-05-17</TIMEX3>
  <TEXT>This case report <EVENT eid="e1" class="OCCURRENCE" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">describes</EVENT> the <EVENT eid="e2" class="OCCURRENCE" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">condition</EVENT> of a 30-year-old man <EVENT eid="e3" class="OCCURRENCE" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">involved</EVENT> in a motor vehicle <EVENT eid="e4" class="OCCURRENCE" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">collision</EVENT>. Clinical <EVENT eid="e5" class="OCCURRENCE" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">examination</EVENT>, <EVENT eid="e6" class="OCCURRENCE" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">X-rays</EVENT>, and computed tomography <EVENT eid="e7" class="OCCURRENCE" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">scan</EVENT> <EVENT eid="e8" class="REPORTING" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">revealed</EVENT> a posterior hip <EVENT eid="e9" class="OCCURRENCE" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">dislocation</EVENT> with an ipsilateral femoral head and mid-shaft fractures. The patient was <EVENT eid="e10" class="OCCURRENCE" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">treated</EVENT> by closed <EVENT eid="e11" class="OCCURRENCE" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">reduction</EVENT> of hip dislocation <EVENT eid="e12" class="OCCURRENCE" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">using</EVENT> a temporarily applied external fixator followed by intramedullary <EVENT eid="e13" class="OCCURRENCE" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">nailing</EVENT> of the femoral shaft. Achieving a closed <EVENT eid="e14" class="STATE" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">reduction</EVENT> is a <EVENT eid="e15" class="STATE" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">challenge</EVENT> with ipsilateral fractures but it should be <EVENT eid="e16" class="STATE" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">favored</EVENT> over open <EVENT eid="e17" class="STATE" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">reduction</EVENT> due to a lower risk of <EVENT eid="e18" class="STATE" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">complications</EVENT>. The type of femoral head <EVENT eid="e19" class="OCCURRENCE" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">fracture</EVENT>, in this case, may have <EVENT eid="e20" class="OCCURRENCE" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">aided</EVENT> in an easier <EVENT eid="e21" class="OCCURRENCE" comment="e3c:permanence=FINITE">reduction</EVENT>.&#13;
</TEXT>
